{
 "cells": [
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# Lesson 6: Electrochemical Cells and Electrodes\n",
    "We have a conceptual framework and the vocabulary to go with it. Before building on it we will detour into the practical world and look at the apparatus that these reactions actually happen in. The choices made here decide what you are able to measure at all.\n",
    "\n",
    "Today's topics:\n",
    "- Galvanic and electrolytic cells\n",
    "- Two electrode and three electrode configurations\n",
    "- Choosing working, reference and counter electrodes\n",
    "\n",
    "**Reference:**\n",
    "- Sections 1.1 and 1.4 in *Electrochemical Methods: Fundamentals and Applications*, Allen J. Bard & Larry R. Faulkner.\n",
    "---"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Configure Environment"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 1,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import sys\n",
    "if '../Resources' not in sys.path:\n",
    "    sys.path.append('../Resources')     # so that echem.py can be found\n",
    "\n",
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
    "from ipywidgets import interact\n",
    "import ipywidgets as widgets\n",
    "from IPython.display import display\n",
    "import schemdraw                        # NEW! for drawing circuit diagrams\n",
    "import schemdraw.elements as elm\n",
    "\n",
    "import echem                            # the shared toolkit for this course\n",
    "from echem import R, T, F, f           # physical constants\n",
    "echem.use_style()                       # course-wide graph formatting"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# 1. Electrochemical Cells \n",
    "\n",
    "Much of the terminology and analysis surrounding these cells also borrows heavily from electric circuits. \n",
    "\n",
    "Electrochemical cells rely upon circuits to deliver or measure the desired current or voltage. These cells can be classified as either:\n",
    "- **Galvanic Cells**: electron transfer reaction is spontaneous. Work can be done to some external system.\n",
    "- **Electrolytic Cells**: electron transfer reaction is not spontaneous. Work must be done to the electrochemical system to drive the reaction."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Two Electrode Cells\n",
    "\n",
    "Because of the need to combine two half-cell reactions, the simplest electrochemical cell possible is a **two-electrode cell**. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 2,
