   "source": [
    "three_electrode_circuit = schemdraw.Drawing(inches_per_unit=0.75)\n",
    "short = three_electrode_circuit.unit/2\n",
    "\n",
    "three_electrode_circuit.add(elm.Line().right())\n",
    "three_electrode_circuit.add(elm.MeterA().up())\n",
    "three_electrode_circuit.add(elm.Line().left())\n",
    "three_electrode_circuit.add(elm.SourceControlledV().left())\n",
    "three_electrode_circuit.add(elm.Line().left())\n",
    "three_electrode_circuit.add(elm.Line().down())\n",
    "three_electrode_circuit.add(elm.Line().right())\n",
    "three_electrode_circuit.add(elm.Dot(open=True))\n",
    "three_electrode_circuit.add(elm.Gap().right().label('').length(short))\n",
    "three_electrode_circuit.add(elm.Gap().down().label('').length(short))\n",
    "three_electrode_circuit.add(elm.Plug(open=True).up())\n",
    "three_electrode_circuit.add(elm.Gap().down().label('').length(1.))\n",
    "three_electrode_circuit.add(elm.Line().down().length(1.6))\n",
    "three_electrode_circuit.add(elm.MeterV().left().length(2.25))\n",
    "three_electrode_circuit.add(elm.Resistor().left().length(2.25))\n",
    "three_electrode_circuit.add(elm.Line().up())\n",
    "\n",
    "# annotate diagram\n",
    "fig = three_electrode_circuit.draw()\n",
    "fig.plot([-4, -4, 1, 1, -4],[1, -1,- 1, 1, 1], fill=(0,0,1,0.05)) # reaction cell\n",
    "fig.plot([-2., -2.],[-1, 1], ls=':', color='gray') # membrane\n",
    "fig.ax.annotate('separator membrane', (-3., 1.25), color='gray')\n",
    "fig.plot([0., 0.],[0.5, -0.5]) # counter/auxiliary electrode\n",
    "fig.ax.annotate('counter\\nelectrode', (1.2,-0.5))\n",
    "fig.ax.annotate('working\\nelectrode', (-5.25,-.5))\n",
    "fig.ax.annotate('reference\\nelectrode', (-1.35,-1.75))\n",
    "\n",
    "fig.ax.scatter([-2.5],[0.7], facecolor='none', edgecolor='red', marker='o', s=200) # cation\n",
    "fig.ax.annotate('ions', \n",
    "            xy=(-2.5, 0.7), xycoords='data', \n",
    "            xytext=(-1.5, 0.7), textcoords='data',\n",
    "            fontsize=10, arrowprops=dict(arrowstyle='<-', color='red')\n",
    "               )\n",
    "fig.ax.scatter([-1.5],[0.25], facecolor='none', edgecolor='blue', marker='o', s=200) # anion\n",
    "fig.ax.annotate('ions', \n",
    "            xy=(-1.5, 0.25), xycoords='data', \n",
    "            xytext=(-2.85, 0.15), textcoords='data',\n",
    "            fontsize=10, arrowprops=dict(arrowstyle='<-', color='blue')\n",
    "               )\n",
    "\n",
    "fig.ax.annotate(r'$A \\rightleftharpoons B + e^-$', (-5.5, 1.6), fontsize=12)\n",
    "fig.ax.annotate(r'$E^0_1, k_{1,a}, k_{1,c}$', (-5.5, 1.25), fontsize=12)\n",
    "fig.ax.annotate(r'$?? \\rightleftharpoons ?? $', (0.8, 1.6), fontsize=12)\n",
    "fig.ax.annotate('uncontrolled', (0.8, 1.25), fontsize=12)\n",
    "\n",
    "fig.ax.annotate('ammeter', (3.25, 0.75), fontsize=12)\n",
    "fig.ax.annotate('high impedance', (-5.8, -2.5), fontsize=12)\n",
    "fig.ax.annotate('voltmeter', (-3.25, -2.25), fontsize=12)\n",
    "fig.ax.annotate('power supply', (-2.3, 3.5), fontsize=12)\n",
    "\n",
    "display(fig)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The circuit in a three electrode cell contains two distinct portions.\n",
    "1. **Working electrode (WE) - reference electrode (RE)**: measures voltage of WE relative to RE, no current flow permitted\n",
    "2. **Working electrode (WE) - counter electrode (CE)**: a power supply passes current between the WE and CE to alter the voltage at the WE"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#### Reference Electrode\n",
    "- acts as an energy **reference point** (similar to TMS in NMR experiments)\n",
    "- must be stable throughout the experiment\n",
    "- connected through a high impedance element such that negligible current flows to/from it"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#### Working Electrode\n",
    "- electroactive species at the solution-electrode interface\n",
    "- can examine solvated species, electrode surfaces, thin films, self-assembled monolayers, etc."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#### Counter (auxiliary) Electrode\n",
    "- a secondary half-cell reaction acts as a **source or sink for electrons**\n",
    "- must be able to supply anodic/cathodic currents at suitable magnitude\n",
    "- voltage applied can be monitored but is not regulated. **Crazy chemistry can happen!**"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "<div class=\"alert alert-block alert-info\">\n",
    "<b>Activity:</b> The counter electrode is unregulated, and the note above warns that <b>crazy chemistry can happen</b> there.\n",
    "<br>\n",
    "You are reducing CO$_2$ at a copper working electrode in aqueous bicarbonate, using a platinum counter electrode. Write down what you think is happening at that counter electrode, and then say how it could contaminate your working electrode.\n",
    "</div>\n",
    "\n",
    "- Reaction at the counter electrode: ANSWER\n",
    "- How it reaches my working electrode: ANSWER\n",
    "- What I would do about it: ANSWER"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#### Benefits of three electrode cells\n",
    "- Isolates the working electrode to enable direct analysis of half cell reactions\n",
    "- anodic or cathodic reactions at the working electrode can be studied\n",
    "- enables spatial separation of WE and CE to minimize contamination\n",
    "- provides effective strategies to minimize how **solution resistance** affects measured voltages"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#### Disadvantages of three electrode cells\n",
    "- More complex apparatus and electrical equipment requirements\n",
    "- Need to maintain reference electrodes\n",
    "- Apparent simplicity belies experimental complexity, which can lead to false confidence in results"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# 2. What can these cells look like?\n",
    "\n",
    "Electrochemical cells range from nanoscale in dimensions to large enough to necessitate industrial facilities.\n",
    "\n",
    "<img src='../Resources/images/cells.png'>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# 3. Electrodes\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Working Electrodes\n",
    "So far, the working electrode has been an arbitrarily defined surface. The material selected for use would be an ideally polarizable material that simply accumulates charge and transfers electrons when needed, but this does not exist. Mercury electrodes were extensively used in the development of electrochemistry because they come close to this behavior *for cathodic reactions*. Mercury is very easily oxidized, however, making it useless for reactions requiring anodic potentials. Early physical electrochemistry was therefore dominated by cathodic reactions, simply because mercury was the easiest way to obtain good data. That habit shaped how the field discussed reactions, but the convention itself was formalized much later: the 1953 IUPAC meeting in Stockholm adopted the reduction convention we use today.\n",
    "\n",
    "All electrode materials have their own properties that should be considered when selecting:\n",
    "- Ease of oxidation: metal oxides or hydroxides may form on the surface, or metal ions may dissolve upon oxidation\n",
    "- Double layer capacitance varies by material\n",
    "- Side reactions: carbon-based electrodes typically have redox active oxygen functionality\n",
    "- Specific interactions/reactions with electrolyte solutions\n",
    "- Specific interactions/reactions with the molecules being analyzed\n",
    "\n",
    "The **electrode operating window** is the voltage region in which a material can be used to study arbitrary processes in solution. A classic figure published in 1984 summarizes historically important examples:\n",
    "\n",
    "<img src='../Resources/images/Potential_Window.jpg' width=40%>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#### Reference Electrodes\n",
    "\n",
    "Sample data has been displayed with the voltage axis in units of **volts vs. something**. That \"something\" is the thermodynamic equilibrium of a well-behaved half-cell reaction. The half-cell reaction is often enclosed into a container of some sort to serve as a **reference electrode**. All the rules of electrochemistry apply to reference electrodes: composition and purity of electrolyte solutions, cleanliness of electrode surfaces, absence of impurities, as well as temperature are important aspects that should be considered. Reference electrodes are easily damaged and are prone to \"drift\", meaning that tender loving care must be exercised!\n",
    "\n",
    "There exists a large number of possibilities, including gases (eg, H$_2$), solvated molecules (eg, ferrocene),  and solid state materials (eg, Silver-Silver Chloride). All reference electrodes can be referenced to the global standard: the **normal hydrogen electrode (NHE)**:\n",
    "\n",
    "$\n",
    "2H^+ + 2e^- \\rightleftharpoons H_2\n",
    "$\n",
    "\n",
    "$E^0 = 0 V$\n",
    "\n",
    "The designated value of $E^0$ does not mean that $\\Delta G^0 = 0$, but that this is the accepted standard reference point. Remember, electrochemical reactions are discussed in terms of half-cell reactions. All reactions are therefore relative. The reference point on the energy scale does not inherently matter, as long as a consistent reference point is used throughout a calculation.\n",
    "\n",
    "Note that the NHE electrode is an experimentally measurable value. Related concepts are the **standard hydrogen electrode (SHE)**, which is the theoretical equivalent of NHE, and the **reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE)**, which takes aqueous solution pH into consideration (more on this in a future lesson).\n",
    "\n",
    "A sample of reference electrodes and their potentials:\n",
    "\n",
    "<img src='../Resources/images/referenceelectrodes.PNG' width=40%>\n",
    "\n",
    "[Table Source](https://solarchemist.se/2016/11/24/reference-electrodes)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#### Counter Electrodes\n",
    "\n",
    "Counter electrodes are the source and/or sink of electrons to enable the electrochemical reaction being studied at the working electrode. The primary requirement is therefore that they are able to supply or accept electrons at the rate that is demanded by the chemistry at the working electrode surface. There should also be little risk of contaminating the solution. Platinum is the historic standard counter electrode material. It can be used in almost any shape or format, but is often used in a format that has a high surface area:\n",
    "\n",
    "<img src='../Resources/images/counterelectrodes.png' width=30%>\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Geometry of the electrochemical cell should also be considered. Remember that the voltage is measured between the WE-RE in a three-electrode cell, but current flows between WE-CE. The electrochemical double layer is therefore driven by ion transfer between these two operating electrodes. Extreme asymmetry in shape and size can lead to non-uniform behavior at the working electrode."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "<div class=\"alert alert-block alert-danger\">\n",
    "<b>Memory Check:</b> A reference electrode must pass negligible current. Using the Nernst equation from [Lesson 4](L04-Conceptual-Framework.ipynb), explain what would happen to its potential if it did pass current.\n",
    "</div>"
   ]
  }
 ],
